FILED s543p (Opus 5.5, 2026-11-27, CODE 0ab08642 = MAIN-READY-111, v2-16300) by the Rosetta census (docs/rosetta-plan.md step 1; #2104).

THE SHAPE (neutral reproducer, run on the tree, perl 5.40.3 vs PCL):

    my @b = (10, 11, 12, 13, 14);
    $_ = "abc";
    my $v = $b[length];
    print "rvalue: $v\n";               # perl 13               PCL 10
    $b[length] = 99;
    print "assign: @b\n";               # perl 10 11 12 99 14   PCL 99 11 12 13 14
    $b[length] .= "x";
    print "append: @b\n";               # perl 10 11 12 99x 14  PCL 99x 11 12 13 14
    $_ = 1; $b[abs] = 77;
    print "abs-assign: @b\n";           # perl 10 77 12 99x 14  PCL 77 11 12 13 14

The interpolation path is RIGHT: `print "$b[length]\n"` (and `$b[shift]`, `$b[ord(...)]`, `$b[pop @w]`, a user sub `$b[two]`) are identical to perl.

WHERE: the emitted Lisp is `(p-aref @b "length")` / `(p-aref @b "time")`.  Pl/PExpr.pm `_bareword_subscript_autoquotes` (~6473) returns 1 ("unknown bareword: string index") for a name that is neither a constant nor a known/prototyped sub -- and a BUILTIN is neither, so the expression path (`_parse_subscript_ix` via `_subscript_autoquote_text`) stringifies it.  perl autoquotes HASH subscripts only; in an array subscript a builtin name is always the builtin.  #246 is the same function's eval-string half (a lowercase user sub inside eval text) -- one fix site, two tasks: a fix for one should probe the other.

CHEAP DISCRIMINATING MEASUREMENT (taken): spelling the argument (`$b[length $_]`) makes the program identical to perl -- the only cause in it.

THE PROGRAM: 0129-Bioinformatics-Global-alignment buckets candidate strings by their length into an array indexed by a bare `length`; every candidate lands in slot 0, so a longer candidate is printed beside the shortest.

=== s543q (Opus 5.5, 2026-11-28, CODE 6a1301ed) -- ROSETTA: 1093-Priority-queue ===
One more program, census B (LOUD by first complaint, which is #5511): `sub insert { push @{ $bins[shift] }, pop }` -- the subscript `shift` is autoquoted to the string "shift" (index 0), so every item lands in one bin and comes out in insertion order.
Neutral reproducer, run on the tree:
    my @a;
    sub f { $a[shift] = pop }
    f(2, "x");
    print scalar(@a), "\n";
    perl: 3     PCL: 1          (the emission is `(p-setf (p-aref @a "shift") (p-pop @_))`)
CHEAP DISCRIMINATING MEASUREMENT (taken): 1093 with #5511 patched and `$bins[shift()]` is byte-identical to perl; with only #5511 patched it prints the items in insertion order.
